withLabelsCode.web:
// The word for each of the five states, and the exit code template.
//
// All five exist because a command goes through all of them: it queues, runs,
// gets interrupted by the reader, finishes or breaks. Stopped is not a
// failure — it is Ctrl-C — and that is why the two words are different.
//
// The template is a template because the word that introduces the number
// belongs to the language, and the number is data. It does not judge the
// number: some commands return one without having failed.
const rotulos = {
  status: {
    idle: 'Queued',
    running: 'Running',
    stopped: 'Stopped',
    complete: 'Finished',
    failed: 'Failed',
  },
  exitCode: 'exit code {code}',
};

extensibilityCode.web:
// THE EXIT CODE ONLY EXISTS AFTER THE END, and the shared vocabulary answers
// that — not a test written on screen.
isRunFinished('running');   // false → no exit code yet
isRunFinished('stopped');   // true  → stopped by a person is an ending too
isRunFinished('complete');  // true
isRunFinished('failed');    // true

// AND THE NUMBER DECIDES NOTHING. Zero means success by convention, but some
// commands return 1 without having failed: one searches and finds nothing,
// another compares and finds a difference. What says what happened is the
// state, which comes from whoever ran it.
//
// Deriving one from the other would give two sources of truth that can
// disagree — the same defect the vocabulary avoids by making the token total a
// function rather than a field.

customizationCode.web:
.nds-terminal-block {
  /* The output ceiling is a custom property, and it is in `rem` on purpose:
     that way it grows with the browser font instead of squeezing more lines
     into the same space when someone enlarges the text. */
  --terminal-block-max-block-size: 32rem;
}

/* The dot colour is REINFORCEMENT, not the clue: the word beside it is what
   describes the state. Changing the dot does not change what reaches people
   who listen. */
.nds-terminal-block-dot {
  inline-size: var(--spacing-3);
  block-size: var(--spacing-3);
}

